>"playwright" is default fixture, so you need not define it.

>headless=False -> headed mode....you can see what is going on the browser

>"page" fixture used chromium engine in headless mode by default. But when using this you can not do some customisations like switching between 2 contexts.

>CSS Selector: #id or .classname or tagname(not preferred since there are many tagnames in page)

>Keep imports absolute (never rely on relative .. imports in test suites).

>Run pytest from the project root or parent folder.

>Use pytest.ini or pyproject.toml to enforce consistent paths across local dev, PyCharm, and CI/CD.

>Keep __init__.py files empty unless you want to expose shortcuts.

>Put shared fixtures in conftest.py at the package root → pytest auto‑discovers them.

>Use session scope for static data (like credentials, configs).

>Keep fixtures modular: one fixture per responsibility (credentials, browser, API client, etc.).

>Avoid hard‑coding paths → always resolve relative to __file__.

>for multiproject setup:
    -One pytest.ini per project → isolates test discovery.
    -Run "pytest" command from project root or specify the project folder explicitly if running from parent folder(PycharmProjects).
    e.g. pytest PythonPlaywright
    -Use conftest.py per project for fixtures local to that project.
    -CI/CD pipelines run pytest per project, not globally.

>ide level run mode changes wont be considered if you are running tests from command line

>"-m" stands for marker expression
add markers such as smoke, regression and register them in pytest.ini
to run particular marker tests use below cmd command
pytest -m smoke
You can combine marker expressions:
pytest -m "smoke or regression" → runs both smoke and regression tests.
pytest -m "smoke and not regression" → runs tests marked as smoke but excludes regression.

>"-k" is a keyword expression filter.
to run particular tests with particular keyword in function names, class names, or test file names)
Below command also executed test_javascript_injection_of_sessionCookies.py may be because it is in Network_interception folder
pytest -k network
more examples:
pytest -k "abc or xyz" → runs tests with "abc" or "xyz" in their names.
pytest -k "abc and not xyz" → runs tests with "abc" but excludes those with "xyz".

>"-n" no. of workers
running tests in parallel
needs external package pytest-xdist to be installed.
Bellow command will run 3 tests in parallel
pytest -n 3
Parallel execution works best when tests are independent/atomic (no shared state, no reliance on order).
pytest -n auto → automatically uses as many workers as CPU cores.

>"--html" -> to generate html report
needs external package pytest-html to be installed.
below command will generate report.html in reports folder. The additional CSS/JS is saved in assets folder.
pytest --html=reports/report.html
Extra options:
--self-contained-html → embeds CSS/JS directly into the report so it’s portable (no external assets).
--html=report.html --css=style.css → lets you apply custom styling.
e.g. pytest --html=reports/report.html --self-contained-html
pytest --html=reports/report.html --self-contained-html -k network
below is pytest.ini setup that automatically generates these HTML reports every time you run tests, so you don’t have to type the flag manually
# pytest.ini
[pytest]
addopts = --html=reports/report.html --self-contained-html
Here addopts → adds default command‑line options for every pytest run so you dont have to specify them separately each time while running tests.

>"--tracing" -> to record the traces
Available options to record a trace:
'on-first-retry' - Record a trace only when retrying a test for the first time.
'on-all-retries' - Record traces for all test retries.
'off' - Do not record a trace.
'on' - Record a trace for each test. (not recommended as it's performance heavy)
'retain-on-failure' - Record a trace for each test, but remove it from successful test runs.
e.g. pytest --tracing retain-on-failure --html=reports/report.html --self-contained-html
after execution launch https://trace.playwright.dev/ and open trace.zip folder

>pytest-bdd
-pytest-bdd implements a subset of the Gherkin language to enable automating project requirements testing and to facilitate behavioral driven development.
-pytest-bdd is a test framework plugin for pytest that allows teams to implement Behavior-Driven Development (BDD) by executing Gherkin human-readable feature files directly within Python. Unlike standalone BDD runners like behave or Cucumber, it integrates seamlessly with the full power, ecosystem, and plugins of standard pytest.
-Test scenarios are written in Gherkin “.feature” files using plain language. Each Given, When, and Then step is “glued” to a step definition – a Python function decorated by a matching string in a step definition module.
This means that there is a separation of concerns between test cases and test code. Gherkin steps may also be reused by multiple scenarios.
-feature can be considered as test suite which can contain multiple scenarios.
e.g. Feature "blog" can contain multiple scenarios related to blog
-install required plugin for your IDE to recognise file as feature file.(show in different icon in explorer. not sure if really necessary?)
-Scenario Outline: used when there is some data need to be passed

>jenkins (CI/CD)
-always download the latest LTS(Long term support) version and .war file(generic java package)
-Check Hardware and Software requirements section and install the required java version for above LTS
-got to .war file location install jenkins with below command
java -jar jenkins.war --httpPort=9090
here we have mentioned to run jenkins server on port 9090 while running on local. you can mention any port.
-in Build steps, select execute shell and give the same cmd command to run playwright tests
e.g. pytest --tracing retain-on-failure --html=reports/report.html --self-contained-html
-Even though a Freestyle job doesn't have a Jenkinsfile, Jenkins stores the entire job configuration as an XML file behind the scenes. You can download this configuration directly via the URL:
https://your-jenkins-server.com/job/Your-Job-Name/config.xml
http://localhost:9090/job/PythonPlaywright_all_tests/config.xml
-parameterize the jenkins job(command) with choice parameter($<parameter_name>). It will then appear in dropdown when you do build with parameters.
e.g. you can parameterize the markers with choice options such as smoke, functional, regression, frameworktests, negative, billing etc.
pytest --tracing retain-on-failure -m "$marker" --html=reports/report.html --self-contained-html
-Schedule the jenkins job with build triggers. You can choose to run Build Periodically.
e.g. 0 9 * * *      -> this will run job everyday 9am and 0 minutes
From left to right, the five fields represent:
MINUTE: 0 (exactly on the hour) or H (a random minute within the hour. in above example anytime between 9:00 AM and 9:59 AM)
HOUR: 9 (9 AM in 24-hour format)
DAY OF MONTH: * (every day)
MONTH: * (every month)
DAY OF WEEK: * (every day of the week)
-Handling Multiple Different Schedules
# Schedule 1: Daily Smoke Tests at 9 AM
H 9 * * * %ENV=production;SUITE=smoke

# Schedule 2: Weekly Full Regression on Fridays at 11 PM
H 23 * * 5 %ENV=staging;SUITE=full_regression

# Schedule 3: Daily frameworktests Tests at 10 AM
H 10 * * * %marker=frameworktests







